# Complete ML Pipeline for Each Algorithm — Headings Only

For your dataset, `Loan Sanction Amount (USD)` is a numerical target, so use regression algorithms.

## 1. Common steps for every algorithm

| Step                       | What to use                                        | Why / when                                                                         |
| -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1. Load data               | Pandas                                             | Read the dataset into a DataFrame.                                                 |
| 2. Separate X and y        | `drop()` and target column                         | X = input features; y = target to predict.                                         |
| 3. Identify column types   | `select_dtypes()`                                  | Separate numerical and categorical columns.                                        |
| 4. Handle missing values   | `SimpleImputer`                                    | Use when columns contain missing values.                                           |
| 5. Encode categorical data | `OneHotEncoder` / `OrdinalEncoder`                 | Convert text categories into numbers.                                              |
| 6. Scale numerical data    | `StandardScaler` / `MinMaxScaler` / `RobustScaler` | Needed for algorithms sensitive to feature scale.                                  |
| 7. Split data              | `train_test_split()`                               | Keep test data for final evaluation.                                               |
| 8. Select algorithm        | Regressor class                                    | Choose the model based on the problem.                                             |
| 9. Hyperparameter tuning   | `GridSearchCV`                                     | Find the best parameter combination.                                               |
| 10. Train and predict      | `fit()` and `predict()`                            | Train the model and predict target values.                                         |
| 11. Evaluate               | `R2`, `MAE`, `MSE` / `RMSE`                        | Measure regression performance.                                                    |
| 12. Compare models         | Cross-validation scores                            | Compare performance fairly.                                                        |
| 13. Significance testing   | Paired t-test / Wilcoxon / Friedman                | Use when you need to test whether score differences are statistically significant. |
| 14. Find best model        | Highest R² or lowest error                         | Choose based on the evaluation metric.                                             |

Important: Fit imputers, encoders and scalers using training data only. Use a `Pipeline` and `ColumnTransformer` to avoid data leakage during cross-validation. If your practical requires no Pipeline, perform the preprocessing after splitting and fit transformations on training data only.

## 2. Algorithm-wise pipeline

The preprocessing steps differ depending on the algorithm.

### A. KNN Regressor

Use when predictions depend on nearby, similar data points.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify numerical and categorical columns
3. Handle missing values
4. One-hot encode categorical columns
5. StandardScaler or MinMaxScaler for numerical columns
6. Train-test split
7. `KNeighborsRegressor`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `n_neighbors`, `weights`, `p`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### B. Support Vector Regression (SVR)

Use for regression with linear or nonlinear relationships.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. StandardScaler for numerical features
6. Train-test split
7. `SVR`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `kernel`, `C`, `epsilon`, `gamma`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### C. Linear Regression

Use as a simple baseline when the relationship is approximately linear.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. Scale numerical features if useful
6. Train-test split
7. `LinearRegression`
8. Fit and predict
9. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### D. Ridge Regression

Use linear regression with L2 regularization to reduce large coefficients.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. StandardScaler
6. Train-test split
7. `Ridge`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `alpha`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### E. Lasso Regression

Use L1 regularization; it can shrink some coefficients to zero.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. StandardScaler
6. Train-test split
7. `Lasso`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `alpha`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### F. Decision Tree Regressor

Use when relationships may be nonlinear and easy-to-follow decision rules are useful.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. Scaling usually not required
6. Train-test split
7. `DecisionTreeRegressor`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `max_depth`, `min_samples_split`, `min_samples_leaf`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### G. Random Forest Regressor

Use an ensemble of decision trees for more stable predictions.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. Scaling usually not required
6. Train-test split
7. `RandomForestRegressor`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `n_estimators`, `max_depth`, `min_samples_leaf`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### H. AdaBoost Regressor

Use an ensemble that focuses progressively on difficult-to-predict observations.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. Scaling usually not required for its usual tree-based base estimator
6. Train-test split
7. `AdaBoostRegressor`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `n_estimators`, `learning_rate`, `estimator`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### I. Gradient Boosting Regressor

Use boosted trees to model complex relationships.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. Scaling usually not required
6. Train-test split
7. `GradientBoostingRegressor`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `n_estimators`, `learning_rate`, `max_depth`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### J. XGBoost Regressor

Use gradient-boosted trees for complex tabular datasets.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. Scaling usually not required
6. Train-test split
7. `XGBRegressor`
8. `GridSearchCV` — tune `n_estimators`, `max_depth`, `learning_rate`, `subsample`
9. Predict
10. Evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

### K. Polynomial Regression

Use when a linear model is too simple and curved relationships are expected.

1. Separate X and y
2. Identify column types
3. Handle missing values
4. Encode categorical columns
5. Apply `PolynomialFeatures`
6. Scale generated numerical features
7. Train-test split (before fitting transformations)
8. `LinearRegression` or `Ridge`
9. Tune polynomial degree if required
10. Predict and evaluate using R², MAE, RMSE

## 3. Final comparison pipeline — after training the models

1. Collect each model's cross-validation scores using the same folds and metric.
2. Compare two paired models using a paired t-test or Wilcoxon, as appropriate.
3. Compare three or more models on the same folds using Friedman when a non-parametric comparison is appropriate.
4. Use ANOVA for suitable independent groups with parametric assumptions; it is not automatically interchangeable with Friedman.
5. If the overall test is significant, use an appropriate post-hoc test to identify which models differ.
6. Select the best model using the highest mean R² or lowest mean MAE/RMSE.
7. Evaluate the selected model on the held-out test set once.

